<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="polyisoprenoid biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWYQT-4449" dataSource="PlantCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Information:&lt;/b&gt; Polyisoprenoid alcohols, such as polyisoprenols, are ubiquitous (even though they can be minor components) in membranes of most living organisms from bacteria, plants and mammals. They are hydrophobic linear polymers, consisting of up to twenty isoprene residues or a hundred carbon atoms (or many more in plants especially), linked head-to-tail, with a hydroxy group at one end (&amp;alpha;-residue) and a hydrogen atom at the other (&amp;omega;-end). In, polyprenols the &amp;alpha;-residue has a double bond, which distinguishes them from the |FRAME:DOLICHOL|s (or dihydropolyprenols) where the double bond in the &amp;alpha;-residue is hydroxylated. Polyisoprenoid alcohols are further differentiated by the geometrical configuration of the double bonds into three subgroups: (i) &amp;omega;-di-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-poly-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;alpha;, &amp;omega;-tri-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-poly-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;alpha; (rubber) (both subgroups are represented by the |FRAME:CIS-TRANS-POLYISOPRENYL-PP| branch of this pathway), and &amp;omega;-all-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;alpha; (|FRAME:TRANS-POLYISOPRENYL-PP| branch). For many years, it was assumed that polyprenols were only present in bacteria and plants, especially photosynthetic tissues, whereas dolichols were found in mammals or yeasts, but it is now known that dolichols can also occur at low levels in bacteria and plants and polyprenols in animal cells. Within a given species, components of one chain-length may predominate, but other homologues are usually present. The chain length of the main polyisoprenoid alcohols varies from 11 isoprene units in eubacteria, to 16 or 17 in &lt;i&gt;Drosophila&lt;/i&gt;, 15 and 16 in yeasts, 19 in hamsters and 20 in pigs and humans. In plants, the range is from 8 to 22 units, but some species of plant have an additional class of polyprenols with up to 5000 units (rubbers |CITS:[10803889]|). In tissues, polyisoprenoid alcohols can be present in the free form, esterified with acetate or fatty acids, phosphorylated or monoglycosylated phosphorylated (various forms), depending on species and tissue. In &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; and many other higher plants, one of the most common &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-polyisoprenol is |FRAME: SOLANESYL-PYROPHOSPHATE|, which contains nine isoprene units and is essential in the biosynthesis of plant quinones, such as |FRAME:UBIQUINONE-9| (UQ-9) and |FRAME:PLASTOQUINONE-9| (PQ-9) |CITS:[12437513],[ROBINSON83]|. Plastoquinone and ubiquinone are known to be major prenylquinones in plants. Plastoquinone is essential for photosynthetic electron transfer in the chloroplast and ubiquinone is essential for respiratory electron transfer in the mitochondrion. Both quinones have the isoprenylated tail in the quinone structure moiety and the length of the prenyl side chain differs between species. Most plants possess nine or 10 units of the isoprenoid side chain of plastoquinone and ubiquinone: &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; contains PQ-9 and UQ-9. Two polyprenyl diphosphate synthases catalyzing this reaction have been identified in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; (SPS1 and SPS2). SPS1 was shown to catalyze the formation of a range of polyprenyl diphosphate products when incubated in the presence of |FRAME: DELTA3-ISOPENTENYL-PP| and |FRAME:FARNESYL-PP| or |FRAME:GERANYLGERANYL-PP| |CITS:[15653808]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="tissier" />
  </Authors>
